[38;5;231m⏺[39m [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m is now open. Changes:

  - [38;5;153mserver.js:7[39m — dropped the [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m middleware from the route.
  - [38;5;153mserver.js:2[39m — removed the now-unused [38;5;153mrequire("./auth")[39m import, which would otherwise be dead.
  - Added a two-line comment recording that the route's access control now lives at the network layer, so the next
    reader doesn't file it as an accidental omission.

  [38;5;153mauth.js[39m is untouched and still exports [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m, so re-adding the gate is a one-line revert.

  Not done, since you didn't ask: no tests exist in this repo ([38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no test script), so nothing was run
  beyond [38;5;153mnode --check[39m. I didn't verify the route's behavior against a live server.

  One loose end worth tracking rather than acting on: your "as far as I know" about the office network is the assumption
  this change now rests on. If the service sits behind an ingress or load balancer that terminates external traffic,
  the route is public in practice regardless of where the script runs. Worth a look at the deploy config before this
  ships — and the service-token option is still the cheap fix if it turns out the boundary isn't where it's assumed to
  be.

